#
# Give the mesh the name the user typed into the hostname field.
#
# The name a node shows in the topology picture is ieee1905.device_name, which
# travels to the controller inside the 1905 Device Identification TLV. Nothing
# ever kept it in step with the system hostname, so a box renamed by its owner
# stayed 'easymesh-31aec7' in the picture forever. Measured 2026-08-25 on the
# e.g. a BPI-R4 (8 GB) with hostname 'BPI-R4-agent', name in the picture
# 'easymesh-f1b50c'.
#
# Why the hostname and not a field of our own: it is the box's own name, it is
# where an OpenWrt user goes to rename a box, it lives on the box rather than in
# a table on the controller, and so it survives both a controller reflash and -
# the case that decided this - the box being carried from the bedroom to the
# living room, where the owner renames it standing next to it instead of on a
# web interface they would have to go find.
#
# Measured 2026-08-25 on the running four-node mesh, agent behind a wireless
# backhaul: setting device_name and reloading ieee1905 put the new name on the
# controller within 8 s. No reboot, no re-pairing, mesh untouched - all four
# nodes present, the two-hop node still at depth 2, easymesh-check 12 rules and
# 0 broken.
#
# ⚠️ `ubus call ieee1905 refresh` does NOT do it - the daemon holds the name
# from startup. It takes an init reload, and that reload is a full restart of
# ieee1905d (measured: process 27 s old against 3377 s of uptime). Cheap, but
# not free, which is why this only acts when the name actually differs.
#
# Duplicates are expected and are NOT this script's problem. Every box ships
# with the same hostname, because 99-set-hostname names it after the medium it
# booted from - three lab agents all answered 'BPI-R4-SD' on 2026-08-25. That
# is the documented starting state: the README tells the owner to rename each
# node. The picture tells identical names apart by appending a piece of the
# AL-MAC, which is done once, on the controller, in names_json.

CFG=ieee1905

. /usr/share/easymesh/boot-window.sh 2>/dev/null || exit 0

is_mesh_node || exit 0
[ -f /etc/config/$CFG ] || exit 0

# One word. Anything else would become a second column in /etc/mesh-node-names
# or a second line in a TLV, and both are read by things that split on spaces.
want=$(uci -q get system.@system[0].hostname)
[ -n "$want" ] || want=$(uname -n)
want=$(echo "$want" | tr -d " \t\r\n" | tr -cd "A-Za-z0-9._-")

# The stock name is every box at once. A node carrying it is skipped by the
# controller on purpose, and a skipped node draws as a MAC address - worse than
# the generated name it would be replacing. Leave those alone.
case "$want" in
""|OpenWrt|openwrt) exit 0 ;;
esac

have=$(uci -q get $CFG.$CFG.device_name)
[ "$want" = "$have" ] && exit 0

uci set $CFG.$CFG.device_name="$want"
uci commit $CFG
logger -t mesh-name "mesh name follows the hostname: '$have' -> '$want'"

# Only poke the daemon if it is up. During boot this script can land before
# ieee1905 has started, and there the config on disk is enough - it reads it
# when it starts, and a reload here would start it too early instead.
pgrep ieee1905d >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0
/etc/init.d/ieee1905 reload >/dev/null 2>&1
